True to a point. Here's the problem with the argument. When the
populations are skewed heavily in favor of one strategy in relation to
another, the tendency is to attribute to causality. In netack the
argument is always trotted out that serial ascenders always level up
normally. The causal argument is then that levelling up normally must be
better because serial ascenders use that strategy. Another off example
is that Microsoft Windows must be the best OS because most everyone uses
it.

The problem with each is that there may not be a direct causal link
between the action and the outcome. To counter example if mrivan had
used the PR for 25 straight ascensions, would that then prove that PR is
superior for ascending? Or if IBM had reached an agreement with Gary
Kildall for CPM for the IBM PC in 1980, is it then the best OS?
Speculative I know, but it shows the point I'm trying to make.

You need more data to make the assertion that something is better. For
example if it could be shown that 90% of players on NAO ascend every
time they level up normally, then that would be significant. But clearly
that's an unprovable point.

